{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1350642059#5_3126850636","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage is dedicated to team coaching and directly answers how coaches can improve team performance, stating that coaches partner with teams on everyday work challenges, introduce new ideas, identify opportunities to improve performance, and build skills through coaching conversations, clarity about performance/development, and team learning.","learned":"Team coaches can improve performance by working with the team in real work challenges, introducing new ideas, facilitating team learning, clarifying performance and development, and helping members gain insight and practice different behaviors."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3374","reason":"This query produced a strong directly relevant team-coaching result; continue the list to find more grade-3 evidence on improving team performance through team coaching."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3374"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1350642059#1_3126842540","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3374","rank":2,"snippet":"Team Coach As with individual coaching, team coaching should focus on results. An effective leadership coach will work with the team to use its relationships, shared experiences, and interpersonal awareness in a way that supports the team’s goals. Many leaders are happy to leave team coaching to professional consultants. If that’s you, in addition to the skills and perspectives needed for one-on-one coaching, you should expect these 5 attributes from a team coach: A Focus on the Whole A team coach may have the ability to coach individuals in many ways, but the goal is to facilitate learning for the team as a whole. The coach should find ways for team members to gain insight and practice different behaviors in the context of the team and its goals. Individual assessment and feedback may be a component of <cover>team coaching, but it’s always related to improving overall team effectiveness.</cover>\" , \"start_char\": 437.000000 , \"title\": \"The Dynamics of Team Coaching - What is Team Coaching? | CCL\" , \"url\": \"https://www.ccl.org/multimedia/podcast/the-dynamics-of-team-coaching/\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"10/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1350642059#1_3126842540","relevance":3,"reason":"This is a dedicated team-coaching source explaining that effective coaches improve team effectiveness by focusing on results, using relationships/shared experiences/interpersonal awareness to support team goals, facilitating whole-team learning, and helping members gain insight and practice new behaviors.","learned":"Effective team coaching improves performance by focusing on team results, strengthening relationships and interpersonal awareness, facilitating learning for the whole team, and tying feedback to overall team effectiveness."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q3374","reason":"The current team-coaching query is yielding directly relevant grade-3 evidence, so continue to collect more results from this productive list."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3374"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1350642059#4_3126848652","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3374","rank":3,"snippet":"within at least 3 relational units: with individual coachees, with the team as a whole, and with the organization. A Long-Term View Team coaching doesn’t always have immediate results. Other business and organizational demands are great and constant, so a coach shouldn’t pressure the group to change too much too soon. If a team coach is persistent and patient, the team and the individuals within it will function more effectively. By partnering with the team in the context of its everyday work challenges, the coach can introduce new ideas and see opportunities to <cover>improve team performance. How to Provide Team Coaching </cover>at Your Organization Being a team coach requires a wide range of skills and perspectives. Underlying those are personal qualities of courage, risk-taking, and maturity. Leaders can build their own coaching skills and learn how to have coaching conversations with their teams, in effect becoming a team coach for their organizations.\" , \"start_char\": 2196.000000 , \"title\": \"The Dynamics of Team Coaching - What is Team Coaching? | CCL\" , \"url\": \"https://www.ccl.org/multimedia/podcast/the-dynamics-of-team-"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"11/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1350642059#4_3126848652","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment of the dedicated CCL article on team coaching. It directly supports how coaches improve team performance: take a long-term, patient approach, work with individuals, the whole team, and the organization, partner with the team on everyday work challenges, introduce new ideas, and identify opportunities to improve performance.","learned":"Repeated CCL evidence: team coaches improve performance by patiently partnering with the team in daily work, considering relationships at individual/team/organization levels, introducing new ideas, and finding improvement opportunities."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"team coaching\" \"team effectiveness\" (+ goals relationships feedback))","reason":"Avoid over-collecting near-duplicate CCL segments and search for additional dedicated team-coaching sources explaining how coaches improve team effectiveness through goals, relationships, and feedback."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"team coaching\" \"team effectiveness\" (+ goals relationships feedback))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1350642059#2_3126844754","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3375","rank":2,"snippet":"leave team coaching to professional consultants. If that’s you, in addition to the skills and perspectives needed for one-on-one coaching, you should expect these 5 attributes from a team coach: A Focus on the Whole A team coach may have the ability to coach individuals in many ways, but the goal is to facilitate learning for the team as a whole. The coach should find ways for team members to gain insight and practice different behaviors in the context of the team and its goals. Individual assessment and <cover>feedback may be a component of team coaching, but it’s always related to improving overall team effectiveness. </cover>A Systems-Thinking Perspective Coaches must understand the complex organizational dynamics in which the team operates. Comfort With Ambiguity Team dynamics often create unpredictability. Coaches shouldn’t expect to drive the direction and specific outcomes of the team. Instead, they must be willing to learn the ways in which the team works and then coach accordingly. The Ability to Set Boundaries Coaches need to be skilled at understanding, identifying, and managing boundaries.\" , \"start_char\": 1065.000000 , \"title\": \"The Dynamics ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_1350642059#1_3126842540","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
