{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"coaching leadership\" \"team performance\" (+ improve improves improving))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_1281645415#0_1951350537","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3380","rank":1,"snippet":"functioning teams High functioning teams defined A model for enabling high functioning teams Order of time “If I have seen further than others, it is by standing upon the shoulders of giants” – Isaac Newton Calling “Your skill is only as great as your passion” – Phil Best, virtuoso pianist Play “In the long run, work does not work without play” – Prof. Stuart Brown Unique place “What you exclude, you attract” – John Whittington, systemic facilitator Shared purpose Balance of exchange Final Thoughts \" , \"segment\": \"High functioning teams — A model for <cover>improving team performance Enabling high functioning teams by Harsha | Apr 19, 2017 | Coaching, Leadership | </cover>2 comments What makes high functioning teams? To answer this question, we first need to recognise what we are dealing with at a very basic level — human beings who have come together from varied walks of life to form a relationship system. A system of interacting and interconnected experiences, families of origin, unique skills, hopes and dreams. Significant inherent complexity is at play and all in one place — the team. It is no surprise then that high functioning teams tend to be the exception to the norm. So, what enables a high functioning "}}
